  "prediction": "**Technology Workforce and Equal Employment Opportunity**\n\nThe technology sector has major employment hubs across the country, with significant concentrations in the San Francisco Bay area, the greater New York City region, and the Washington-Arlington-Alexandria region. Technology workers are employed in various industries, including the technology sector and outside of it. The technology workforce has grown at a higher rate than the general workforce, with an estimated 7.5 million workers in 2015, up from 5.3 million in 2005.\n\nDespite the growth, the technology workforce remains predominantly male and White, with women and minorities underrepresented. In 2015, women made up only 22% of the technology workforce, compared to 48% in the general workforce. Minority workers, including Black, Hispanic, and Asian workers, also face underrepresentation in the technology sector.\n\nThe Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) and the Office of Federal Contract Compliance Programs (OFCCP) are responsible for enforcing equal employment opportunity laws and regulations in the technology sector. The EEOC received over 91,000 charges of employment discrimination in 2016, with a significant number related to technology companies. The OFCCP conducts compliance evaluations and investigations to ensure that federal contractors, including technology companies, comply with affirmative action and nondiscrimination requirements.\n\nThe report makes several recommendations to improve oversight of technology companies and contractors, including:\n\n1. The EEOC should develop a timeline to complete the planned effort to clean IMS data for a one-year period and add missing industry code data.\n2. The OFCCP should analyze internal process data to better understand the cause of delays in compliance evaluations and make changes accordingly.\n3. The OFCCP should require contractors to disaggregate demographic data for setting placement goals in affirmative action plans.\n4. The OFCCP should evaluate its approach to selecting contractor establishments for compliance evaluation and ensure that it reflects current workplace structures and locations.\n5. The OFCCP should assess the usefulness of the Functional Affirmative Action Program as an alternative to establishment-based programs.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the need for increased diversity and inclusion in the technology workforce and for improved oversight and enforcement of equal employment opportunity laws and regulations in the technology sector.",
